e experience  corners of the planet that meant that  they might otherwise never get to see  from the tops of the highest mountains  to the depths of the oceans and of  course along the way  we brought all those experiences on  immobilizes as well giving users  everywhere a trusted guide to the real  world that they can bring with them  anywhere to help them get where they're  going fast and to find the best places  to visit along the way to build these  sorts of powerful user experiences we've  had to also build out an incredibly rich  and complex understanding of the real  world and it turns out that the  underlying data that powers these  consumer mapping experiences can also  fuel some incredibly powerful enterprise  use cases as well today we have over  three million developers and ten over  10,000 paying customers using our Maps  API is to do everything from visualizing  points on a map to routing and planning  for deliveries to doing things like  pre-programming TV remotes based on  their location and looking forward we're  building on these capabilities and  bringing together all of our both  historical and real-time knowledge about  the world combining it with things like  machine learning and our club computer  code compute capabilities and using that  to provide business with even more  insights and predictive capabilities so  today I want to share with you three key  ways that we're seeing enterprises  currently take advantage of our Google  Maps data and services to help grow  their businesses and also share some  examples of what we think is possible  together today we see clearly how Google  Maps can help you build better  experiences for your customers how it  can help you optimize your business and  how it can help enable smart strategic  decisions so I talked earlier today  about how we're constantly seeking ways  to make our consumers experiences better  with Google Maps and we see that same  appetite from our enterprise partners  finding ways to use the power of Google  Maps to deliver better experiences to  their end users for example we see auto  companies like Audi Tesla and  you leveraging our local business  listings and their internet systems to  help their drivers easily fine and get  to millions of businesses and points of  interest around the world and we've also  helped transform how how people are  getting around today by doing things  like powering the base maps and  navigation experiences for companies  like uber lyft and others in retail  we're helping companies like Crate &amp;  Barrel Target and Best Buy connect  online actions to offline foot traffic  and in doing so helping them to drive  more feet into their stores and we see  savvy online sellers also you doing  things like taking advantage of our  autocomplete capabilities to make it  easier for the users to type an address  into their shopping cart it does things  like lead directly to fewer abandoned  carts and increase conversion rates let  me talk about a fun one for a minute if  you remember going to Disney World and  getting a giant complicated paper map of  the park and it's crowded you're trying  to figure out how do I get to space now  no no no if I go there right now is the  line gonna be super long right Disney's  a super fun place that can also be  overwhelming and incredibly frustrating  and so Disney Parks had this grand  vision of building an in part and park  app experience that would let you do  everything from understand what the wait  times were to make reservations for the  rides you wanted to go to and help you  easily get around anywhere in the park  well it turns out that to do these  things well one of the things you need  is an incredibly detailed highly -  highly detailed digital map of every  corner of Disney and so several years  back we embarked on a long-term  multi-year partnership with Disney to  create just that and as we got into it  along the way we discovered that there  was a lot more we could do beyond that -  fun things like enabling contextual  location-based experiences so if you've  got a son or a daughter who's really  into goofy you can get a notification in  real time that tells you when goofy  happens to be nearby we also did things  like incorporating all the impart  transit systems and lighting up  turn-by-turn directions so now you can  literally find the fastest way to get to  Splash Mountain right now whether it's  walking or taking them on a rail so it's  a really fun project for us a great  partnership  and we're super pleased to have helped  make the happiest place on earth just a  little bit happier for all of disneys  many visitors let's talk about another  phone when briefly you just heard about  pokemons go from Phil and crew and their  stories are sort of phenomenal example  of what's possible today for consumer  experiences leveraging Google Maps right  pokey [ __ ] took our mapping data and  build an incredible augmented reality  game on top of it and if their early  success is any indication we fully  expect that we're gonna see a lot more  fun and interesting augmented reality  experiences coming in the near future  both for consumers and for enterprises  here's another one as the Internet of  Things scales up location services are  becoming even more essential for  creating smart experiences I mean I  don't know about you but who woulda  thought a few years ago that everything  from your thermostat to your light bulb  to your refrigerator is going to be  connected and can be able to be  controlled by your smartphone but it's  not so crazy to think about in fact it's  not so crazy at this point to think  about a world in the not-too-distant  future where literally every device in  your household is connected in one way  or another and so imagine how cool will  be if your light switches and  thermostats know that you're about to  pull into your driveway and it's new  coming home not your kids and so it can  turn on your lights and the air  conditioning according to your  preferences right before you get home or  knowing your car's about to pull into  the driveway your alarm and your door  lock and automatically disarmed so you  can carry your groceries into the house  without having to put them down all  right in a connected world location  awareness can fuel all sorts of new and  fun ways to interact with the things in  our physical surroundings that gives you  a very brief taste of some of the many  ways that we are seeing businesses build  really game-changing experiences for  their customers on top of our data and  insights about the world so the second  big area that we're seeing major  innovation from enterprises is in  optimizing their core business one of  our core strengths and maps is  increasingly becoming our understanding  of real-time traffic data and many  businesses especially those that are in  the business of moving things in the  world tap into this rich data to help  streamline their operation  and optimize their workforce so from  transport and logistics companies to  things like emergency relief and  disaster response we're seeing more and  more organizations putting maps at the  center of their operations and helping  them more efficiently locate manage and  monitor their resources it's one example  fleet minder used the Google Maps API is  to build are starting to map their  vehicle locations so their dispatchers  could view where they were and send them  real-time updates as things were  changing and what we saw from them is  that is that this drove direct savings  that went right to their bottom line so  to give you an example their CIO has  told us that by using Google's mapping  solutions it's helped them cut their  inefficient driving in half and has  helped reduce incorrect deliveries that  they were providing by 20% but now build  on little build on that a little further  and let's imagine what's possible when  that real-time traffic understanding can  get baked much more deeply into your  systems or as we're beginning to be able  to do also predict what traffic or  movement might look like under different  conditions in the real world in the  future imagine being able to ask things  like how many extra taxis should I have  at 5:00 p.m. in New York on a Thursday  when the Yankees are playing or can I  guarantee a 15-minute delivery window to  my customers when there's a Beyonce  concert happening in LA on Thursday  night  these are increasingly the types of  questions that customers are asking us  to help them with they want to optimize  them route their fleets better based on  changing conditions in the real world  and we're really excited to be giving  more and more businesses the insights  that they need to flexibly respond to  real time real world conditions  finally our deep understanding of  location combined with machine learning  in the cloud provides incredible  potential to help enterprises make smart  strategic decisions an example is a  German insurance company called wgv  insurance and they created a risk map on  top of our data to understand both where  their policyholders are and help them  model with different risk management  scenarios so in less than six weeks they  were able to take their policyholder  information  overlay it together with things like  flood areas weather reports and other  types of geo data to help them make  better decisions about how to  financially model their business and I  think it's a testament to the power of  this sort of data and tooling that they  were able to fundamentally shift how  they did risk assessment modeling in  such a short period of time we also see  a lot of businesses asking questions  about the real world that I that they  don't have answers to or that they're  spending very very heavily on physical  infrastructure to answer and this is a  big area where our understanding of how  the world moves can help influence  important commercial decisions out there  in the physical world so give you one  example we're currently in conversations  with a major city that traditionally has  spent hundreds of millions of dollars on  things like putting sensors in their  roads are sending surveys out to homes  to find out things about traffic and  commuting patterns and we're talking to  them now about how they can instead use  our real-time traffic data machine  learning and predictive analytics to  help make urban planning decisions  easier faster more accurate and very  importantly far far less expensive it's  just one example of how our rich data  and analytics capabilities can really  drive change and the way companies go  about gathering information about  physical movement in the world just two  weeks ago we announced the acquisition  of urban engines who built a location  analytics business around use cases like  transit and transportation we want to be  able to help answer questions like where  are the where are the next areas of  urban sprawl going to be and can we use  that information to decide where to  build the next highway or where I should  put my next retail store or even how  much retail staff should I plan to have  on hand next Friday night based on what  the predicted level of foot traffic  nearby is going to be we're really  excited about the new opportunities that  this deep location intelligence can fuel  building on top of this rich  comprehensive understanding of the  physical world and how it moves over the  last decade Google Maps has made some  incredible strides and yet I really  quite honestly feel like we're just at  the beginning of something new  even more exciting it's particularly  exciting to me that the types of  problems that businesses are looking to  us to help them solve are exponentially  more challenging than they were even a  few years back you know a few years ago  most of the conversations we were having  the businesses around things like how  can we help them visualize the real  world how could we do things like show  some custom data on top of a map but  today enterprises are increasingly  asking us about much more sophisticated  location problems ones that involve  planning spending insights movements  predictions in essence we're helping  businesses move beyond just visualizing  the world and helping them make major  real world decisions and at the center  of it all is location and it's  understanding how the real world moves  so you've heard today the combination of  our raw location understanding and  movement data from the billion users of  our consumer products combined with  Google's machine learning and cloud  compute power can change the game very  very quickly for a lot of industries  from retail to real estate  transportation to logistics and more so  if you or your customers operate in the  physical world then location should  matter deeply to you and if it does we  invite you to talk to us what fuels our  imagination more than anything our  real-world problems and we love to  invite you all to a conversation with us  about what your business needs are and  the problems that you're most looking to  solve we're just at the beginning of  helping businesses unlock the potential  from this understanding of location and  we're very excited to partner with all  of you to bring these opportunities to  life thank you

